    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 141\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 2493, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to providing a tax credit\n      for universal visitability; and providing for the repeal  of  such\n      provisions upon expiration thereof\"\n \nAssembly Bill Number 7475, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  tax  law and the labor law, in relation to\n      establishing a small business tax credit  for  the  employment  of\n      disabled  persons; and providing for the repeal of such provisions\n      upon expiration thereof\"\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 4398, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the tax law, the insurance law  and  the  executive\n      law,  in  relation  to  enacting  the  residential  structure fire\n      prevention act of 2019\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  Each of these bills would implement a  costly  new  incentive  program\nimpacting  all  New  York  taxpayers. One would provide an incentive for\nrepairing potential fire hazards in certain homes; another would provide\nan incentive to employers to hire disabled persons;  and  the  last  one\nwould provide an incentive to a homeowner to alter their home to make it\nmore  readily accessible to anyone with limited mobility. The incentives\nprovided to the taxpayers would be tax credits that range from $2,750 to\n$25,000.\n \n  Programs such as these that have large scale fiscal implications  must\nbe  discussed  in the context of the budget process. It is irresponsible\nto have an agreement on a fiscal plan in April, only to then pass  these\nmeasures and provide no source of funding, leaving taxpayers on the hook\nand  shortchanging existing programs. While each of these proposals have\nmerit and are laudable in their own right,  these  decisions  cannot  be\nmade  in a vacuum. For these insurmountable fiscal reasons, I veto these\nbills, although I welcome the opportunity to discuss these and other tax\ncredits and deductions in the context of the budget.\n \n  The bills are disapproved.                  (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 141,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2019
   }
  ],
